:: Day1_Summons_at_Dawn [warrior_queen_arc day1]

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
/*  THE BLADE REFORGED — Day 1: The Summons at Dawn           */
/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── SCENE 1: THE WAKING ── */

The knock come not as a knock at all, but as a fist upon oak — three strikes, heavy as a headsman's count, splitting the black silence of my chamber like an axe through kindling.

I jolt upright in bed. The sheets pool at my waist, and for one disoriented heartbeat I feel them drag across my chest in a way that send a faint, prickling shiver through me — across skin that has grown tender these past weeks, across the small swellings beneath my nightgown that I have trained myself not to look at, not to touch, not to think upon.

I think upon them anyway.

The knock come again.

<<speech "aldric">>Open this door, Your Grace. Or I shall open it myself, and it shall not enjoy the experience.<</speech>>

That voice. Gravel dragged across iron. I know it as well as I know the sound of my own breathing — though even that has changed of late. Shallower. Quicker. As though my lungs have forgotten how much room they once commanded.

I swing my legs from the bed and my bare feet find the cold stone floor. The nightgown — a thing of pale linen that Veletia had practically forced upon me three nights past, declaring my old sleeping trousers *"an insult to the very concept of rest"* — fall to my mid-thigh. I tug at it as I cross the chamber. It cling to places it ought not cling. The fabric brush against the slight swell of my chest with every step, a whisper of sensation I cannot silence.

I open the door.

Sir Aldric Vornhelm fill the doorframe the way a boulder fill a riverbed — immovably, and with the quiet promise that anything foolish enough to contest the arrangement shall be ground to dust. His scarred face is lit by the single corridor torch behind him, one half in amber light, the other in shadow. His arms are crossed over a chest that could serve as a battering ram. Frostfang hang at his hip, the great broadsword's pommel catching torchlight like a baleful eye.

He look at me.

I become acutely, miserably aware that I am standing before the Commander of the Royal Guard in a woman's nightgown, barefoot, with my hair — longer now, past my jaw, unruly with sleep — falling across one eye.

His gaze travel downward for precisely one second. Then back up. His expression do not change. But something in his jaw tighten, the way a man's jaw tighten when he has bitten into something unexpected and is deciding whether to swallow or spit.

<<speech "aldric">>Get dressed. Training clothes. Iron Garden. You have the time it take me to walk there.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Aldric, the sun has not even—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>The sun rise when it please. I rise when the kingdom demand it. So shall you.<</speech>>

He turn on his heel and march down the corridor, his boots striking the stone in a rhythm as precise and unforgiving as a war drum.

I stand in the doorway, the cold air raising gooseflesh along my bare legs — legs that are thinner than they were a month ago, smoother, the muscle beneath them leaner and longer. I glance down. The nightgown's hem sway against my thighs. My thighs, which now press together at the top when I stand with my feet close, a soft contact that was not there before, a warmth between them that is new and strange and impossible to ignore when one is standing half-naked in a cold corridor at an ungodly hour.

I close the door and lean against it.

My heart is hammering. Not from fear of Aldric — though only a fool would not fear Aldric — but from the look in his eyes. That single downward glance. He had *seen* it. The changes. The narrowing of my shoulders, the soft beginning of curve at my waist, the way the nightgown draped over a silhouette that no longer match the one he has trained for ten years.

He had seen it, and he had said nothing, and somehow that is worse than any words.


/* ── SCENE 2: DRESSING ── */

I cross to the wardrobe and pull it open. The left side hold my old clothes — tunics and breeches cut for a prince's frame. The right side, added only recently by Veletia's insistence, hold newer garments. Training clothes that have been... adjusted.

I reach for a familiar tunic and pull it on.

It do not fit.

The shoulders sag where once they filled. The chest hang loose everywhere except — *there*. Two small points where the fabric catch and pull taut, outlining what is growing beneath with a clarity that make my stomach drop. I cross my arms over my chest instinctively, pressing the fabric flat, but the moment I release, it spring back to its traitorous shape.

I tear it off and stand for a moment, bare from the waist up, arms at my sides. The mirror beside the wardrobe catch me before I can look away. My chest — two slight mounds, no larger than halved plums, the skin around them flushed pink and threaded with faint veins that were not there a week ago. The nipples... different. Wider. Darker. They pucker in the cold air and a shiver run through them that I feel all the way to my spine. Not pain. Something worse. *Sensitivity*. Like every nerve in that new flesh has been tuned to a frequency I never asked to hear.

I cover myself with my hands. My own palms feel strange against them — the skin so soft it's almost spongy, yielding beneath even gentle pressure, and beneath that yielding, a firmness. Something *growing*. Building itself from nothing, cell by cell, pushing outward against my hands as though to say: *You cannot hold me back. I am coming whether you wish it or not.*

I pull my hands away and reach for one of the newer tunics — a darker fabric, thicker weave, with a slightly gathered front that Veletia had designed to *"accommodate the journey,"* as she put it with that infuriating theatrical wink. It fit better. The chest is... handled. Not hidden, precisely, but managed. The waist is tapered in a way my old clothes never were, and when I smooth the fabric down I feel the shape of myself through it — the inward curve above the hip that was not there two months ago, the narrowing that make my torso look less like a column and more like... well. Like a woman's torso. An early draft of one, anyway.

Breeches next. I pull them on and — they are tight at the hip. Tighter than last week. I have to tug and shift, working the fabric over a pelvis that has widened by a degree I can feel in my very bones, a spreading ache deep in the joint that Thalara tell me is *"the cradle reshaping itself."*

The cradle. As though my body is making room for something I cannot bring myself to name.

I fasten the breeches and the waistband dig into flesh that has softened, a thin layer of padding across my lower belly that wasn't there before — not fat, exactly, but a cushioning, a feminine distribution that Thalara say is the body preparing itself. For what, she did not specify. She did not need to.

I lace my boots. Even these feel different — my feet have lost perhaps half a size. The boots are slightly loose. I stuff a bit of cloth into the toes as I have taken to doing and curse softly.

I catch my reflection one final time as I move toward the door. The figure in the mirror is lean, angular still, but softened at every edge — like a blade that someone has begun to grind into a different shape. The jaw. The neck. The way the tunic fall. The flare at the hip where once there was a straight descent from rib to thigh.

I look like a boy wearing a woman's bones.

Or a woman wearing a boy's stubbornness.

I leave the mirror to its judgments and step into the corridor.


/* ── SCENE 3: THE WALK TO THE IRON GARDEN ── */

The castle is dark and silent at this hour. My boots echo against stone — a sound that is lighter than it used to be. I weigh less. My stride is shorter. I catch myself walking with my elbows closer to my body, my steps more contained, as though my frame is instinctively folding inward, occupying less space.

When did I start walking like this?

Two chambermaids round the corner ahead of me, carrying armfuls of linen toward the laundry. They see me and stop. One curtsy — then hesitate, her eyes flickering over my shape in the dim torchlight, clearly uncertain whether the bow should be deeper, the kind reserved for ladies of court. The other just stare, her mouth open slightly, taking in the hair, the waist, the way I move.

<<speech "maid_1">>Your... Your Grace. Good morning.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Good morning. Carry on.<</speech>>

My voice. Even that betray me now. Not higher — not yet — but softer at the edges, as though someone has taken sandpaper to the rougher frequencies. I clear my throat and walk past them, and I hear — I *hear* — one whisper to the other:

<<speech "maid_2">>Is that still the prince? Look at the way he... *she*... look at the hips on—<</speech>>

The whisper cut off as I turn the corner. My face burn. My hands clench. And my hips — those traitorous, widening, swaying hips — continue their gentle lateral rhythm with every step, a motion I did not learn and cannot unlearn, born of the changed architecture of my pelvis, the altered attachment of muscle to bone.

I pass the great corridor where the ancestors' portraits hang. Queens, all of them. Velmorian queens stretching back seven hundred years — armored, crowned, fierce-eyed women who ruled with blade and blood. I have walked past these portraits ten thousand times. But this morning, for the first time, I do not see them as ancestors.

I see them as mirrors.

My reflection flash across the glass of Queen Elyria's portrait as I pass. For one terrible, tilting instant, I see a young woman in the glass — dark-haired, uncertain, her body caught between two truths — and I cannot tell where Elyria end and I begin.

I walk faster.

The Iron Garden is the castle's oldest training ground — a walled yard of packed earth and stone, ringed by weapon racks and scarred wooden dummies that have absorbed the fury of a hundred generations of Velmorian warriors. The morning air bite at my skin as I step through the arch. The sky is that bruised blue-grey that precede the dawn, and the stars are fading like sparks dying on an anvil.

Aldric is already there. Of course he is.

He stand in the centre of the yard with his arms behind his back, Frostfang unsheathed and planted point-down in the earth before him. He has the stillness of a man who has been waiting a long time and intend to make someone pay for it.

<<speech "aldric">>You are late.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You gave me the time it took you to walk here.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Aye. And you took longer. That is what late mean.<</speech>>

I bite back a retort and walk to the centre of the yard to stand before him. The morning cold is sharper than I expected. My new skin — thinner, softer, more susceptible to temperature, as I have been learning in a hundred small miserable ways — prickle with gooseflesh beneath the tunic. I feel my nipples tighten against the fabric, those new wider darker nipples that seem to react to *everything* now — cold, friction, even strong emotion — and I pray to every god in every temple that Aldric cannot see them through the weave.

He is not looking at my chest. He is looking at all of me, and that is far worse.


/* ── SCENE 4: THE FIRST TEST ── */

Aldric pull Frostfang from the earth and sheathe it in one fluid motion. Then he walk toward the weapon rack, select a standard training longsword, and toss it to me.

I catch it.

Or rather — I try to catch it. The sword spin once in the air and my hand close around the grip, but the weight pull my arm down farther than I expect. It is the same training sword I have wielded since I was fourteen. It weigh the same it has always weighed. But my arm do not.

<<speech "aldric">>Swing it. Basic overhead, on my count.<</speech>>

I set my feet in the wide stance I have used for a decade — feet shoulder-width apart, weight evenly distributed, blade high. Aldric raise a hand.

<<speech "aldric">>One.<</speech>>

I bring the blade down. The arc is clean, practiced—

— and my right hip *shift*. The wider pelvis, the altered balance point, the subtle redistribution of weight that the potions have wrought — it all conspire in one treacherous instant. My centre of gravity, which has sat in my solar plexus for twenty years, has dropped. Moved lower. Settled somewhere in my gut, maybe lower still. The swing that should have been straight pull to the left, my hips rotating in a way I did not command, and my back foot skid on the packed earth.

I stumble. Not a fall — I catch myself — but a graceless, lurching half-step that would have been fatal against any opponent faster than a sleeping cow.

Aldric watch. His expression is stone.

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

I reset. Plant my feet. Swing.

The same thing. The hip. The rotation. The pull to the left. This time I overcorrect and my upper body lean too far right, and the weight of the sword — gods, why is it so *heavy* — drag me into a full stumble. My knee hit the dirt.

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Aldric, something is—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I know precisely what is. Again.<</speech>>

I rise. I swing. The sword go wide. My shoulders — narrower by two fingers' breadth this month — cannot generate the same rotational force. The torque that once came from broad, thick trapezius muscles and a wide collarbone now meet a frame that is being whittled thinner by the day. The power isn't there. Not the way it was.

I swing again. And again. Each time, something is wrong. The balance. The power. The angle. My body is a house whose foundations have shifted in the night, and every wall hang at an angle that look right but is imperceptibly, catastrophically wrong.

After the tenth swing, Aldric raise his hand.

<<speech "aldric">>Stop.<</speech>>

I stand there, breathing hard — harder than ten swings should warrant, but my lungs are shallower now, my ribcage subtly compressed, and the air come in quick, insufficient gulps. Sweat has already begun to bead at my temples. The tunic cling to my back, and at my chest, the dampness outline the two small mounds with a fidelity that make me want to rip the fabric off and fling it into the nearest fire.

But I cannot rip it off. Not here. Not in front of him. Because beneath that tunic, I am not what I was. The last time Aldric saw me shirtless — sparring three months ago — my chest was flat, hard, a warrior's chest. Now there are... those. And the thought of Aldric seeing them, seeing the soft yielding flesh that has no business on a soldier's body, make something hot and shameful crawl through my gut.

Aldric approach. He stop an arm's length away and look at me the way a mason look at a wall that has cracked — not with anger, but with the cold calculation of a man deciding how much must be torn down before rebuilding can begin.


/* ── SCENE 5: THE ASSESSMENT ── */

<<speech "aldric">>How long since your last potion?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Four days. Thalara administered the torso draught on—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I did not ask for a calendar. I asked because I need to know how much ground I've lost. Four days. The torso draught.<</speech>>

He walk around me. Slowly. His boots crunch on the packed earth and I feel his gaze like a physical thing — tracking the line of my shoulders, the set of my spine, the flare of my hips beneath the breeches.

<<speech "aldric">>Stand straight. Arms at your sides. Don't move.<</speech>>

I obey. And I hate it — hate the vulnerability of it, standing here in the grey dawn like a horse being appraised at market, while the man who trained me to kill inspect the ways in which I have become... less. Or different. Or something he has no training manual for.

<<speech "aldric">>Your shoulders have dropped by two fingers since I last measured you. Here—<</speech>>

He reach out and press two fingers against my left shoulder, against the muscle that caps the joint. The deltoid. It was round and hard three months ago. Now his fingers sink into it slightly — not soft, not weak, but *changed*. The muscle is leaner, longer, redistributed along the bone in a pattern that favor speed over strength.

<<speech "aldric">>—and here.<</speech>>

He move to my arm, wrapping his hand around my bicep. His grip nearly encircle it now. Three months ago, there was no chance of that. I feel his calloused palm against skin that is smoother than it has any right to be, and something flutter in my stomach that I refuse to examine.

<<speech "aldric">>Arms thinner. Not weaker yet, but the mass is redistributing. You've lost perhaps a stone of upper body weight in two months. The potions are moving it... elsewhere.<</speech>>

His gaze drop to my hips. To the breeches that strain at the sides. To the new breadth that I cannot hide and cannot explain to anyone who has not watched their own skeleton slowly reshape itself from the inside out.

<<speech "aldric">>Your waist has drawn in. I can see it even through that tunic, and I suspect you can feel it every time you breathe.<</speech>>

I say nothing. He is not wrong. Every breath is different now. The ribcage is subtly smaller, the intercostal muscles pulling inward, and each inhale feel like breathing through a slightly narrower doorway. Not suffocating. Just... contained. Feminine. A woman's breathing pattern, shallow and quick, driven more by the chest than the belly. I have noticed it at night, lying in bed, my hand on my stomach — the belly barely moves. But my chest rise and fall in a rhythm I do not recognize as mine.

<<speech "aldric">>And your hips.<</speech>>

He pause behind me. I can feel him looking at the change that is, perhaps, the most visible of all — the widening of my pelvis, the way the breeches strain, the new breadth that throw every stance, every step, every movement into disarray.

<<speech "aldric">>Your centre of gravity has dropped three inches at the least. Your old stance — the wide warrior's plant — it is not merely ineffective now. It is dangerous. Your body will fight you every time you try to use it. That stumble just now? In a real fight, that is a sword through your gut.<</speech>>

He come back around to face me. His scarred face is grim, but there is something else in his eyes — not pity, never pity from Aldric — but a kind of severe, reluctant acknowledgment of the magnitude of what I am enduring.

<<speech "aldric">>Let me be plain with you, Your Grace, because no one else in this castle will be. Everything I have taught you — every stance, every guard, every strike pattern, every footwork drill, every instinct I have beaten into your muscle over ten years of training — it is all wrong now. Every last piece of it. Not because you have forgotten, but because the body that learned it no longer exist.<</speech>>

The words land like blows. I feel them in my chest — in the new, tender flesh that ache when struck, in the ribs that are subtly reshaping themselves, in the heart that beat faster and lighter than a prince's heart ought.

<<speech "mc">>Then what am I to do?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>You are to unlearn. And then you are to learn again. From the first step. From the first breath. As though you have never held a blade in your life.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I have trained since I was ten years old. You would have me begin as a child?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I would have you begin as what you are. Which is neither the boy I trained nor the woman you are becoming, but something in between — something that do not yet know its own strength, its own reach, its own limits. And that, Your Grace, is the most dangerous thing a warrior can be.<</speech>>

He let the silence sit for a moment. The first pale fingers of dawn have begun to creep over the eastern wall, painting the tops of the weapon racks in thin gold.


/* ── SCENE 6: THE REFORGING DECLARED ── */

<<speech "aldric">>I am calling it The Reforging.<</speech>>

He say it flatly, the way he say everything — as though the words are hammered iron, shaped for use rather than ornament.

<<speech "aldric">>Starting on the morrow, you will report to this yard at first light. Every day. No exceptions. Not illness, not council, not feast, not the gods-damned end of the world. We will take apart everything you think you know about fighting and we will rebuild it — for the body you have. Not the body you had. Not the body you will have. The one you stand in, right now, today, this hour.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>And if my body change again tomorrow? If the next potion reshape me further?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Then we adjust. A blade must be reforged each time the steel is reworked. That is the nature of what you are doing to yourself. You have chosen to melt down and recast your very form, and I — I must teach a shifting blade how to cut true. It is...<</speech>>

He pause. For a moment, something almost human flicker behind the iron mask of his face.

<<speech "aldric">>It is the most difficult thing I have ever been asked to do. And I have been asked to do many things by many queens.<</speech>>

He look at me — really look, not the mason's appraisal from before, but something deeper, something that see past the changing flesh and the ill-fitting breeches and the sweat-damp tunic to the person underneath. The person who is neither prince nor queen. The person who is afraid.

<<speech "aldric">>But I will do it. Because if I do not, the next assassin, the next battle, the next creature that come out of the dark will find you with a body you do not understand and a fighting style built for a ghost — and you will die. And Velmoria will die with you.<</speech>>

I stare at him. The weight of it — the training, the transformation, the kingdom balanced upon my changing shoulders — press down upon me like a physical force. I feel it in the balls of my feet, in the ache of my widened hips, in the tight trembling of muscles that are no longer shaped for the burdens they carry.

<<speech "mc">>Every day? First light?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Every day. First light. And you will wear what I tell you to wear. Train how I tell you to train. Fight how I tell you to fight. No arguments. No pride. The prince is dead on this training ground, Your Grace. Only the student remain.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>And what of the queen? Is she dead too?<</speech>>

He look at me for a long moment. The dawn light has reached his face now, turning the old scars to ridges of gold and shadow. And then, so quietly I might have imagined it:

<<speech "aldric">>The queen has not been born yet. That is what the Reforging is for.<</speech>>

He turn toward the armoury gate, pausing without looking back.

<<speech "aldric">>First light. Do not make me come to your chamber again. I'll not be so gentle next time.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>That was gentle?<</speech>>

But he is already gone, his silhouette swallowed by the archway, and the sound of his boots fade into the grey corridors of the castle like the last rumble of a passing storm.


/* ── SCENE 7: THE RETURN — THE BODY ALONE ── */

I walk back to my chamber slowly. The castle is waking now — more servants in the corridors, the smell of bread from the kitchen below, the distant clang of the smith beginning his day. People see me and bow or curtsy, and every single one of them hesitate for that half-breath, that tiny recalibration, their eyes making a calculation they probably aren't even aware of: *prince or princess? He or she? What do I call this creature that walk like neither and both?*

I keep my eyes forward. My back straight. My arms close to my body, because when I let them swing naturally they brush against my hips in a way they never used to, the wider pelvis pushing my gait into a rhythm that is conspicuously, undeniably feminine, and I cannot — *cannot* — let them see how much that terrify me.

Inside my chamber, I close the door and lock it. The fire has died to embers. The room is cold. I stand with my back against the oak and breathe — that shallow, quick, woman's breathing I cannot seem to stop — and I feel the day pressing against me from all sides.

I pull off the boots first. Then the breeches — peeling them down over hips that resist the removal, the fabric catching on the new width, requiring me to shimmy and tug in a way that is humiliatingly, specifically feminine. When did undressing become a negotiation?

The tunic next. I pull it over my head and the fabric drag across my chest — across those tender, growing mounds — and a sound escape me. Not quite a gasp. Not quite a moan. Something in between, involuntary and deeply private, the sound of sensitive flesh being stimulated in a way that my body has only recently learned to feel.

I stand in the centre of my chamber in nothing but the smallclothes — the newer ones, the ones Veletia had made, softer fabric, a different cut that curve beneath my lower belly and sit higher on hips that demand the accommodation. I look down at myself.

My body.

This strange, mutinous, half-finished body.

The chest: two small swellings, each perhaps enough to fill a cupped palm, round and firm and flushed with blood from the morning's exertion. The nipples stand erect in the cold, darker and wider than a man's nipples ought to be, ringed by areolae that have expanded like slow-blooming flowers. When I brush my fingers across one — just barely, just testing — a current of sensation shoot from the point of contact down through my torso and settle, warm and confusing, somewhere deep in my pelvis. I jerk my hand away.

The waist: narrower. Visibly, unmistakably narrower, as though someone has pressed their hands against my sides and squeezed. The ribs are drawing inward. The oblique muscles are redistributing. There is a *shape* to my torso now — an hourglass hint, the earliest suggestion of a curve that will only deepen with each potion.

The hips: wider. The breeches left red marks where they pressed against bone that has literally moved, shifted outward, the iliac crest spreading to create a cradle that nature — or alchemy — is preparing for a purpose I am still trying not to think about.

Between my legs: I do not look. Not today. I can feel the changes — the slow recession, the tightening, the reshaping that has begun in earnest since the last potion — but I am not ready to see. Not yet. That confrontation can wait for a day when I have more courage than I do right now, standing half-naked and shivering in a cold chamber while a scarred old soldier's words echo in my skull.

*Everything I have taught you is wrong now.*

I pull on the nightgown. The linen fall against my new skin like a whisper, draping over the small breasts, catching at the nipples, sliding down the curve of my waist and over the swell of my hips. It is a woman's garment and it fit me like it was made for me and that is perhaps the most frightening thing that has happened today, which is saying something, because today has been full of frightening things.

I sit upon the edge of my bed.

The mattress dip beneath me and I feel my thighs press together — that soft, warm contact at the top, flesh against flesh, where once there was a gap. It is such a small thing. Such a tiny, insignificant detail. But it is the detail that break me, right now, in this moment — the proof that I cannot sit, cannot stand, cannot walk, cannot even *be still* without my body reminding me of what it is becoming.

I press my palms against the outside of my thighs and feel the new breadth of them, the way they curve outward from a waist that is pulling inward like a drawn breath. I squeeze gently and the flesh yield beneath my fingers — softer than muscle should be, padded with a layer of feminine subcutaneous fat that the potions are distributing with the precision of a sculptor's hand.

I release. My hands fall to the bed. I stare at the far wall.

<<speech "mc">>First light. Every day.<</speech>>

I say it aloud to the empty room, to the dying fire, to the body that is betraying and remaking me in equal measure. I say it like a soldier reciting an oath before battle. Because that is what this is. A war — not against an enemy, but against the distance between who I was and who I must become.

The blanket pull up to my chin. The fabric slide across my chest — across the tender, swelling flesh that ache faintly with each heartbeat, that press against whatever I wear, that remind me with every breath that the boy who once slept in this bed is becoming someone else.

Sleep do not return.

But somewhere in the darkness, between one heartbeat and the next, I hear Aldric's voice:

*The queen has not been born yet.*

I lie still. I feel the alchemy working in my bones — a deep, tidal pull, patient and relentless, reshaping me cell by cell while the world sleep.

The queen has not been born yet.

But she is stirring.

And come first light, she will learn to fight.


/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
/*  END OF DAY 1                                               */
